1
00:00:03,799 --> 00:00:10,759
The barn was very large. It was very old. It smelled of hay and it smelled of

2
00:00:10,759 --> 00:00:18,679
manure. It smelled of the perspiration of tired horses and the wonderful sweet

3
00:00:18,679 --> 00:00:25,958
breath of patient cows. It often had a sort of peaceful smell, as though

4
00:00:25,958 --> 00:00:30,679
nothing bad could happen ever again in the world.

5
00:00:30,679 --> 00:00:37,159
It smelled of grain and of harness dressing and of axle grease and of

6
00:00:37,159 --> 00:00:42,639
rubber boots and of new rope.

7
00:00:42,679 --> 00:00:47,559
And whenever the cat was given a fish head to eat, the barn would smell of

8
00:00:47,559 --> 00:00:55,399
fish, but mostly it smelled of hay, for there was always hay in the great loft

9
00:00:55,399 --> 00:01:01,559
up overhead, and there was always hay being pitched down to the cows and the

10
00:01:01,559 --> 00:01:05,078
horses and the sheep.

11
00:01:05,078 --> 00:01:09,879
The barn was pleasantly warm in winter when the animals spent most of their

12
00:01:09,879 --> 00:01:16,600
time indoors, and it was pleasantly cool in summer when the big doors stood wide

13
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:18,679
open to the breeze.

14
00:01:18,679 --> 00:01:23,958
The barn had stalls on the main floor for the workh horses, tie-ups on the

15
00:01:23,958 --> 00:01:29,799
main floor for the cows, a sheepfold down below for the sheep, a pig pin down

16
00:01:29,799 --> 00:01:37,399
below for Wilbur, and it was full of all sorts of things that you find in barns.

17
00:01:37,399 --> 00:01:45,078
ladders, grindstones, pitchforks, monkey wrenches, sthes, lawnmowers, snow

18
00:01:45,078 --> 00:01:51,799
shovels, axe handles, milk pales, water buckets, empty grain sacks, and rusty

19
00:01:51,799 --> 00:01:57,480
rat traps. It was the kind of barn that swallows like to build their nests in.

20
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:02,919
It was the kind of barn that children like to play in. And the whole thing was

21
00:02:02,919 --> 00:02:06,679
owned by Fern's uncle, Mr. Homer L.

22
00:02:06,679 --> 00:02:09,480
Zuckerman.

23
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:14,198
Wilbur's new home was in the lower part of the barn, directly underneath the

24
00:02:14,198 --> 00:02:20,120
cows. Mr. Zuckerman knew that a manure pile is a good place to keep a young

25
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:25,799
pig. Pigs need warmth, and it was warm and comfortable down there in the barn

26
00:02:25,799 --> 00:02:29,559
cellar on the south side.

27
00:02:29,559 --> 00:02:32,759
Fern came almost every day to visit him.

28
00:02:32,759 --> 00:02:37,480
She found an old milking stool that had been discarded, and she placed the stool

29
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:40,599
in the sheepfold next to Wilbur's pen.

30
00:02:40,599 --> 00:02:49,318
Here she sat quietly during the long afternoons, thinking and listening and watching Wilbur.

31
00:02:49,318 --> 00:02:55,400
The sheep soon got to know her and trust her. So did the geese who lived with the

32
00:02:55,400 --> 00:03:01,880
sheep. All the animals trusted her. She was so quiet and friendly. Mr. Zuckerman

33
00:03:01,880 --> 00:03:07,079
did not allow her to take Wilbur out, and he did not allow her to get into the

34
00:03:07,079 --> 00:03:12,279
pig pin, but he told Fern that she could sit on the stool and watch Wilbur as

35
00:03:12,279 --> 00:03:18,038
long as she wanted to. It made her happy just to be near the pig, and it made

36
00:03:18,038 --> 00:03:23,558
Wilbur happy to know that she was sitting there right outside his pen. But

37
00:03:23,558 --> 00:03:31,200
he never had any fun. No walks, no rides, no swims.

38
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:37,079
One afternoon in June, when Wilbur was almost 2 months old, he wandered out

39
00:03:37,079 --> 00:03:40,440
into his small yard outside the barn.

40
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:46,440
Fern had not arrived for her usual visit. Wilbur stood in the sun, feeling

41
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:53,798
lonely and bored. "There's never anything to do around here," he thought.

42
00:03:53,798 --> 00:03:58,839
He walked slowly to his food trough and sniffed to see if anything had been

43
00:03:58,839 --> 00:04:05,720
overlooked at lunch. He found a small strip of potato skin and ate it. His

44
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:12,679
back itched, so he leaned against the fence and rubbed against the boards.

45
00:04:12,679 --> 00:04:17,319
When he tired of this, he walked indoors, climbed to the top of the

46
00:04:17,319 --> 00:04:23,640
manure pile, and sat down. He didn't feel like going to sleep. He didn't feel

47
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:30,919
like digging. He was tired of standing still, tired of lying down. "I'm less

48
00:04:30,919 --> 00:04:37,079
than 2 months old and I'm tired of living," he said. He walked out to the

49
00:04:37,079 --> 00:04:43,960
yard again. "When I'm out here," he said, "there's no place to go but in.

50
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:50,599
When I'm indoors, there's no place to go but out in the yard."

51
00:04:50,599 --> 00:04:56,119
That's where you're wrong, my friend. My friend, said a voice. Wilbur looked

52
00:04:56,119 --> 00:05:01,160
through the fence and saw the goose standing there. You don't have to stay

53
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:06,359
in that dirty little dirty little dirty little yard, said the goose, who talked

54
00:05:06,359 --> 00:05:12,679
rather fast. "One of the boards is loose. Push on it. Push push push on it

55
00:05:12,679 --> 00:05:17,000
and come on out." "What?" said Wilbur.

56
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,440
Say it slower.

57
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:26,359
At the risk of repeating myself, said the goose, I suggest that you come on

58
00:05:26,359 --> 00:05:33,960
out. It's wonderful out here. Did you say a board was loose? That I did that I

59
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:39,798
did, said the goose. Wilbur walked up to the fence and saw that the goose was

60
00:05:39,798 --> 00:05:49,798
right. One board was loose. He put his head down, shut his eyes, and pushed

61
00:05:50,359 --> 00:05:56,038
The board gave way. In a minute, he had squeezed through the fence and was

62
00:05:56,038 --> 00:06:01,319
standing in the long grass outside his yard.

63
00:06:01,319 --> 00:06:08,599
The goose chuckled. "How does it feel to be free?" she asked. "I

64
00:06:08,599 --> 00:06:15,720
like it," said Wilbur. "That is I guess I like it." Actually, Wilbur felt queer

65
00:06:15,720 --> 00:06:21,960
to be outside his fence with nothing between him and the big world. "Where do

66
00:06:21,960 --> 00:06:26,759
you think I'd better go?" "Anywhere you like, anywhere you like," said the

67
00:06:26,759 --> 00:06:33,240
goose. Go down through the orchard. Root up the sod. Go down through the garden.

68
00:06:33,240 --> 00:06:37,000
Dig up the radishes. Root up everything.

69
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,599
Eat grass. Look for corn. Look for oats.

70
00:06:40,599 --> 00:06:47,640
Run all over. Skip and dance. Jump and prance. Go down through the orchard and

71
00:06:47,640 --> 00:06:51,000
stroll in the woods.

72
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:56,599
The world is a wonderful place when you're young. I can see that," replied

73
00:06:56,599 --> 00:07:03,480
Wilbur. He gave a jump in the air, twirled, ran a few steps, stopped,

74
00:07:03,480 --> 00:07:10,519
looked all around, sniffed the smells of afternoon, and then set off walking down

75
00:07:10,519 --> 00:07:12,599
through the orchard.

76
00:07:12,599 --> 00:07:17,880
Pausing in the shade of an apple tree, he put his strong snout into the ground

77
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:21,798
and began pushing, digging, and rooting.

78
00:07:21,798 --> 00:07:27,720
He felt very happy. He had plowed up quite a piece of ground before anyone

79
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:33,558
noticed him. Mrs. Zuckerman was the first to see him. She saw him from the

80
00:07:33,558 --> 00:07:39,319
kitchen window and she immediately shouted for the men.

81
00:07:39,319 --> 00:07:49,480
Homer, she cried. Pigs out. Larvy, pigs out. Homer, Larvy, pigs out. He's down

82
00:07:49,480 --> 00:07:56,440
there under that apple tree. Now the trouble starts, thought Wilbur. Now I'll

83
00:07:56,440 --> 00:08:02,759
catch it. The goose heard the racket and she too started hollering. Run, run, run

84
00:08:02,759 --> 00:08:08,759
downhill. Make for the woods. The woods, she shouted to Wilbur. They'll never

85
00:08:08,759 --> 00:08:12,279
never never catch you in the woods.

86
00:08:12,279 --> 00:08:17,480
The cocker spaniel heard the commotion and he ran out from the barn to join the

87
00:08:17,480 --> 00:08:22,679
chase. Mr. Zuckerman heard and he came out of the machine shed where he was

88
00:08:22,679 --> 00:08:28,519
mending a tool. Larvy the hired man heard the noise and came up from the

89
00:08:28,519 --> 00:08:34,119
asparagus patch where he was pulling weeds. Everybody walked toward Wilbur

90
00:08:34,119 --> 00:08:41,880
and Wilbur didn't know what to do. The woods seemed a long way off and anyway

91
00:08:41,880 --> 00:08:48,679
he had never been down there in the woods and wasn't sure he would like it.

92
00:08:48,679 --> 00:08:51,240
Get around behind him, Lurvby, said Mr.

93
00:08:51,240 --> 00:08:56,279
Zuckerman, and drive him toward the barn. And take it easy. Don't rush him.

94
00:08:56,279 --> 00:09:01,640
I'll go and get a bucket of slops. The news of Wilbur's escape spread rapidly

95
00:09:01,640 --> 00:09:07,159
among the animals on the place. Whenever any creature broke loose on Zuckerman's

96
00:09:07,159 --> 00:09:12,839
farm, the event was of great interest to the others. The goose shouted to the

97
00:09:12,839 --> 00:09:16,119
nearest cow that Wilbur was free.

98
00:09:16,119 --> 00:09:24,119
And soon all the cows knew. Then one of the cows told one of the sheep,

99
00:09:24,119 --> 00:09:28,119
and soon all the sheep knew.

100
00:09:28,119 --> 00:09:33,200
The lambs learned about it from their mothers.

101
00:09:33,798 --> 00:09:38,839
The horses in their stalls in the barn pricricked up their ears when they heard

102
00:09:38,839 --> 00:09:45,720
the goose hollering. And soon the horses had caught on to what was happening.

103
00:09:45,720 --> 00:09:52,679
"Wilbur's out," they said. Every animal stirred and lifted its head and became

104
00:09:52,679 --> 00:09:58,359
excited to know that one of his friends had got free and was no longer penned up

105
00:09:58,359 --> 00:10:00,839
or tied fast.

106
00:10:00,839 --> 00:10:06,519
Wilbur didn't know what to do or which way to run. It seemed as though

107
00:10:06,519 --> 00:10:12,440
everybody was after him. If this is what it's like to be free, he thought, I

108
00:10:12,440 --> 00:10:17,400
believe I'd rather be pinned up in my own yard.

109
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:23,079
The cocker spaniel was sneaking up on him from one side. Lurvy, the hired man,

110
00:10:23,079 --> 00:10:28,440
was sneaking up on him from the other side. Mrs. Zuckerman stood ready to head

111
00:10:28,440 --> 00:10:30,679
him off if he started for the garden.

112
00:10:30,679 --> 00:10:37,798
And now Mr. Zuckerman was coming down toward him, carrying a pale.

113
00:10:37,798 --> 00:10:41,319
This is really awful, thought Wilbur.

114
00:10:41,319 --> 00:10:45,480
Why doesn't Fern come? He began to cry.

115
00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:51,400
The goose took command and began to give orders. Don't just stand there, Wilbur.

116
00:10:51,400 --> 00:10:57,960
Dodge about. Dodge about, cried the goose. Skip around. Run toward me. Slip

117
00:10:57,960 --> 00:11:05,880
in and out. In and out. In and out. Make for the woods. Twist and turn.

118
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:13,319
The cocker spaniel sprang for Wilbur's hind leg. Wilbur jumped and ran. Larvy

119
00:11:13,319 --> 00:11:20,038
reached out and grabbed. Mrs. Zuckerman screamed at Lurvy. The goose cheered for

120
00:11:20,038 --> 00:11:26,200
Wilbur. Wilbur dodged between Lurvy's legs. Lurvy missed Wilbur and grabbed

121
00:11:26,200 --> 00:11:29,319
the spananiel instead. "Nicely done!

122
00:11:29,319 --> 00:11:35,000
Nicely done!" cried the goose. "Try it again. Try it again.

123
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:47,000
Run down him, suggested the cows. Run toward me, yelled the gander. Run up hill, cried

124
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:54,440
the sheep. Turn and twist, honked the goose. Jump and dance, said the rooster.

125
00:11:54,440 --> 00:12:02,599
Look out for Lurvy, called the cows. Look out for Zuckermanmen, yelled the gender. Watch

126
00:12:02,599 --> 00:12:11,400
out for the dog, cried the sheep. Listen to me, listen to me, screamed the goose.

127
00:12:11,400 --> 00:12:16,599
Poor Wilbur was dazed and frightened by this hullaloo.

128
00:12:16,599 --> 00:12:21,960
He didn't like being the center of all this fuss. He tried to follow the

129
00:12:21,960 --> 00:12:27,480
instructions his friends were giving him, but he couldn't run downhill and

130
00:12:27,480 --> 00:12:32,679
uphill at the same time, and he couldn't turn and twist when he was jumping and

131
00:12:32,679 --> 00:12:37,880
dancing, and he was crying so hard he could barely see anything that was

132
00:12:37,880 --> 00:12:47,159
happening. After all, Wilbur was a very young pig, not much more than a baby, really.

133
00:12:47,159 --> 00:12:53,000
He wished Fern were there to take him in her arms and comfort him. When he looked

134
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:58,599
up and saw Mr. Zuckerman standing quite close to him, holding a pale of warm

135
00:12:58,599 --> 00:13:02,038
slops, he felt relieved.

136
00:13:02,038 --> 00:13:08,038
He lifted his nose and sniffed. The smell was delicious.

137
00:13:08,038 --> 00:13:14,839
warm milk, potato skins, wheat middlings, Kellogg's corn flakes, and a

138
00:13:14,839 --> 00:13:20,038
popover left from the Zuckermanman's breakfast. "Come, pig," said Mr.

139
00:13:20,038 --> 00:13:27,640
Zuckerman, tapping the pale. "Come, pig!" Wilbur took a step toward the pale.

140
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:34,519
"No, no, no," said the goose. "It's the old pale trick, Wilbur. Don't fall for

141
00:13:34,519 --> 00:13:39,640
it. Don't fall for it. He's trying to lure you back into captivity. He's

142
00:13:39,640 --> 00:13:47,480
appealing to your stomach. Wilbur didn't care. The food smelled appetizing. He

143
00:13:47,480 --> 00:13:54,839
took another step toward the pale. Pig, pig, said Mr. Zuckerman in a kind voice

144
00:13:54,839 --> 00:14:00,519
and began walking slowly toward the barnyard, looking all about him

145
00:14:00,519 --> 00:14:08,839
innocently as if he didn't know that a little white pig was following along behind him.

146
00:14:08,839 --> 00:14:15,240
"You will be sorry, sorry, sorry," called the goose. Wilbur didn't care. He

147
00:14:15,240 --> 00:14:18,839
kept walking toward the pale of slops.

148
00:14:18,839 --> 00:14:24,679
You'll miss your freedom, honked the goose. An hour of freedom is worth a

149
00:14:24,679 --> 00:14:28,839
barrel of slops. Wilbur didn't care.

150
00:14:28,839 --> 00:14:34,200
When Mr. Zuckerman reached the pig pen, he climbed over the fence and poured the

151
00:14:34,200 --> 00:14:39,640
slops into the trough. Then he pulled the loose board away from the fence so

152
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:44,679
that there was a wide hole for Wilbur to walk through.

153
00:14:44,679 --> 00:14:52,038
"Reconsider! Reconsider!" cried the goose. Wilbur paid no attention. He

154
00:14:52,038 --> 00:14:54,839
stepped through the fence into his yard.

155
00:14:54,839 --> 00:15:00,599
He walked to the trough and took a long drink of slops, sucking in the milk

156
00:15:00,599 --> 00:15:04,119
hungrily and chewing the popover.

157
00:15:04,119 --> 00:15:07,558
It was good to be home again.

158
00:15:07,558 --> 00:15:13,480
While Wilbur ate, Lurvy fetched a hammer and some eight penny nails and nailed

159
00:15:13,480 --> 00:15:16,679
the board in place. Then he and Mr.

160
00:15:16,679 --> 00:15:25,000
Zuckerman leaned lazily on the fence and Mr. Zuckerman scratched Wilbur's back with a stick.

161
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:32,839
"He's quite a pig," said Lurvy. "Yes, he'll make a good pig," said Mr.

162
00:15:32,839 --> 00:15:39,640
Zuckerman. Wilbur heard the words of praise. He felt the warm milk inside his

163
00:15:39,640 --> 00:15:46,440
stomach. He felt the pleasant rubbing of the stick along his itchy back. He felt

164
00:15:46,440 --> 00:15:53,480
peaceful and happy and sleepy. This had been a tiring afternoon.

165
00:15:53,480 --> 00:16:00,839
It was still only about 4:00, but Wilbur was ready for bed. "I'm really too young

166
00:16:00,839 --> 00:16:07,839
to go out into the world alone," he thought as he lay down.

167
00:16:09,079 --> 00:16:11,558
Chapter 4.

168
00:16:11,558 --> 00:16:14,000
loneliness.
